{
  "title": "Biceps Subluxation",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3046/biceps-subluxation",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 21 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Biceps subluxation is a recognized cause of anterior shoulder pain usually associated with a subscapularis tear.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be suspected clinically with anterior shoulder pain made worse with provocative tests and confirmed with MRI studies to evaluate for concurrent subscapularis tear.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment involves an initial trial of NSAIDs, activity modification and physical therapy. Arthorscopic versus open biceps tenodesis/tenotomy with or without subscapularis repair is indicated for recurrent symptoms.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most commonly associated with",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**subscapularis tears**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "most common associated pathology",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "disruption of the biceps sling",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Biceps tendon anatomy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "originates off supraglenoid tubercle and superior labrum",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "stabilized by the biceps sling which is comprised of",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3046/images/screen_shot_2019-11-24_at_9.07.02_pm.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**fibers of the subscapularis**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**supraspinatus**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**coracohumeral**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**superior glenohumeral ligaments**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "acts as dynamic stabilizer",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "involved in movement such as shoulder flexion, abduction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Complete glenohumeral anatomy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**anterior** shoulder pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may have sensation of clicking",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam of shoulder",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "strength",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**due to the association with subscapularis tears, strength of the subscapularis muscle should be performed**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "biceps provocation tests",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**Yergason's test**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "anterior shoulder pain with resisted forearm supination with the arm at the side and the elbow flexed to 90 degrees.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**Speed's test**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "anterior shoulder pain with resisted shoulder flexion with the shoulder flexed at 90 degrees, elbow in full extension and the palm facing upwards\"",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**palpable click**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "may be produced with arm abduction and external rotation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "occurs when tendon subluxes or dislocates out of groove",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ultrasound",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3046/images/ultrasound.jpg",
              "caption": "Ultrasound"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "can give **dynamic test** of bicep instability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3046/images/4-26-2012%206-26-33%20pm.jpg",
              "caption": "MRI"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "can show increased T2 signal, and displacement out of the bicipital groove",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**coincides with subscapularis tears**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "decreased angle between the long head biceps to glenoid during arthroscopy has been associated with biceps tendon subluxation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "NSAIDS, PT strengthening, and steroid injections",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "initial management",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "direct steroid injection in proximity, but not into tendon",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "arthroscopic vs open biceps tenotomy vs tenodesis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3046/images/tenodesis.jpg",
                  "caption": "Tenodesis"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "reserved for refractory cases for bicep pathology",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "performed with or without subscapularis repair",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "can test instability intra-operatively",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
